WEBVTT

00:00:00.333 --> 00:00:01.443
Sunlight looks white.

00:00:01.749 --> 00:00:03.179
So why is the sky blue?

00:00:03.464 --> 00:00:05.834
The answer is hiding
inside the light itself.

00:00:06.205 --> 00:00:09.555
White light is really a mix of
every colour, squeezed together.

00:00:09.861 --> 00:00:13.351
When sunlight enters our air,
it hits tiny gas molecules.

00:00:13.817 --> 00:00:16.707
Those molecules bounce the
light in every direction.

00:00:16.937 --> 00:00:18.207
This is called scattering.

00:00:18.543 --> 00:00:21.033
But short waves scatter
far more than long ones.

00:00:21.309 --> 00:00:22.839
Blue light has a short wave.

00:00:23.159 --> 00:00:24.549
Red light has a long one.

00:00:24.765 --> 00:00:27.085
So blue gets thrown all around the sky,

00:00:27.085 --> 00:00:29.975
and reaches your eyes
from every direction.

00:00:30.191 --> 00:00:31.181
What about violet?

00:00:31.522 --> 00:00:32.852
It scatters even more.

00:00:33.142 --> 00:00:37.832
But the sun gives off less of it, the air
absorbs some, and our eyes see it poorly.

00:00:38.173 --> 00:00:39.143
So we see blue.

00:00:39.519 --> 00:00:42.189
At sunset, the light has
to cross much more air.

00:00:42.459 --> 00:00:45.389
Most of the blue is scattered
away before it reaches you.

00:00:45.595 --> 00:00:47.345
What is left is orange and red.

00:00:47.631 --> 00:00:51.901
In 1871, Lord Rayleigh worked
out the maths of all this.

00:00:52.196 --> 00:00:55.546
And no, the sky is not blue
because it reflects the ocean.

00:00:55.852 --> 00:00:59.402
Next time you look up, you are seeing
sunlight, scattered by the air.

